<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="avenacin biosynthesis, initial reactions" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6115" dataSource="PlantCyc" />
  <Description>|FRAME: CPD-10678 Avenacin| is an antifungal saponin in which the aglycones are |FRAME: CPD-6948|-derived pentacyclic triterpenoids esterified with either N-methylanthranilic acid or benzoic acid. The sugar unit is a branched trisaccharide. They are found exclusively in |FRAME: TAX-4496 &quot;&lt;i&gt;Avena&lt;/i&gt; (oat)&quot;| roots and have been implicated in the resistance of |FRAME: TAX-4496| seedlings to the fungal soil pathogen &lt;i&gt;Gaeumannomyces graminis var. tritici&lt;/i&gt;, the causal agent of `take-all&apos; disease of wheat and barley. The pentacyclic triterpenoid part of the avenacins like the tetracyclic triterpenoid ring system of the phytosterols and their conjugates, is formed from mevalonic acid by way of isopentenyl diphosphate, farnesyl diphosphate and 2,3-oxidosqualene. The |FRAME: CPD-10678| and phytosterol pathways then diverge with the cyclization of |FRAME: EPOXYSQUALENE| to form the |FRAME: CPD-6948| needed for |FRAME: CPD-10678| biosynthesis and the cycloartenol needed for phytosterol biosynthesis. The formation of |FRAME: CPD-10678| from |FRAME: CPD-6948| requires: (i) extensive oxyfunctionalization of the pentacyclic triterpenoid nucleus of |FRAME: CPD-6948|, (ii) esterification of the partly or fully functionalized |FRAME: CPD-6948| nucleus with either |FRAME:CPD-402|, which forms |FRAME: CPD-10678| and |FRAME:CPD-16597| or |FRAME:BENZOATE| which generates |FRAME: CPD-16599| and |FRAME:CPD-16598|, respectively |CITS: [10872206 ] [23532069]|.</Description>
